        "Competency Mechanism": "Students develop Problem-Solving & Decision-Making by completing a concrete task where success requires observable behaviors tied to the competency rather than passive conversation.",
        "Observable Behaviors": "identifies options, weighs tradeoffs, chooses a strategy, revises after consequences, explains reasoning",
        "Success Criteria": "Students complete the task or produce a visible artifact, then use evidence from the activity to explain how their choices demonstrated Problem-Solving & Decision-Making.",
        "Materials Needed": "large floor map or printed grid; cones or tape markers; mission tokens; one timer; one recording sheet per student or team.",
        "Preparation": "Print or prepare all materials. Create 3-6 team kits. Pre-set any clues, role cards, boundaries, or model images before students enter. Prepare a visible timer and a simple score or evidence tracker.",
        "Room Setup": "Use tabletop space. Mark clear start, work, and finish zones. Post the mission goal, rules, and success criteria where students can see them. Arrange students in teams of 3-5 unless the activity requires pairs.",
        "Rules": "Students may ask clarifying questions before the timer starts. During play, Guides coach with questions only. Teams must record at least one strategy change between rounds. Safety and respectful participation override speed or score.",
        "Round 1": "Run the base challenge exactly as stated: place solar, wind, park, and housing tiles on a map while meeting citizen needs and budget limits.",
        "Round 2": "Repeat with a required planning pause: teams must name one strategy, role, or communication change before restarting.",
        "Round 3": "Add one constraint such as reduced time, fewer materials, no talking for one minute, or a surprise information card.",
        "Round 4": "Swap roles or perspectives so a different student leads, communicates, records evidence, or tests the solution.",
        "Round 5": "Final transfer round: teams apply what they learned to a new version of the same challenge and attempt to beat their prior outcome.",
        "Guide Coaching Moves": "Notice the target behaviors: identifies options, weighs tradeoffs, chooses a strategy, revises after consequences, explains reasoning. Ask: 'What evidence are you using?' 'What is your next move?' 'Who has not been heard yet?' 'What changed after that attempt?'",
        "Common Failure Modes": "Students rush without planning; one student dominates; teams treat the task as a race only; students skip recording evidence; frustration replaces strategy. Pause and redirect to the competency behavior, not the score.",
        "Debrief Questions": "Describe: What happened during the challenge? Analyze: Which choices helped or hurt your outcome? Relate: Where else will you need Problem-Solving & Decision-Making this week? What would you do differently in a second attempt?",
        "Support Scaffolds": "Provide visual rule cards, sentence stems, smaller team roles, partner preview of materials, reduced number of constraints, or a worked example. Allow drawing or pointing when language load is high, unless communication limits are the core challenge.",
        "Extension Opportunities": "Early finishers add a new constraint, coach another team without touching materials, create a second version of the challenge, or collect data comparing strategies across teams.",
        "Adaptable Elements": "Narrative theme, materials, time limit, team size, number of rounds, and scoring system can change while preserving the core competency mechanism.",
        "Non-Negotiables": "Students must do the task, make decisions during action, experience consequences, and reflect using evidence tied to Problem-Solving & Decision-Making. Do not reduce this to a conversation or worksheet.",
        "Assessment Evidence": "Collect team plan, attempt data, final artifact or score, and individual reflection naming one competency behavior demonstrated and one next step.",
